WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.363
Critical thinking depends on information quality.

2
00:00:03.363 --> 00:00:07.796
A source may sound polished, confident, or popular and still be weak.

3
00:00:07.796 --> 00:00:19.185
Source evaluation asks who produced the information, what expertise they have, what evidence they provide, what incentives may influence them, and what uncertainty remains.

4
00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:21.783
The point is not to distrust everything.

5
00:00:21.783 --> 00:00:25.146
It is to assign confidence in proportion to support.

6
00:00:25.146 --> 00:00:27.592
Credibility is not only about status.

7
00:00:27.592 --> 00:00:29.809
Expertise should match the topic.

8
00:00:29.809 --> 00:00:34.318
A person may be highly qualified in one area and unreliable in another.

9
00:00:34.318 --> 00:00:41.885
Incentives matter because sources may be selling, defending, simplifying, persuading, or trying to win attention.

10
00:00:41.885 --> 00:00:49.987
Conflicts of interest do not automatically make information false, but they should shape how carefully the claim is checked.

11
00:00:49.987 --> 00:00:55.796
Primary sources are closer to the original evidence, data, event, or official statement.

12
00:00:55.796 --> 00:01:00.611
Secondary sources interpret, summarize, or comment on primary material.

13
00:01:00.611 --> 00:01:01.834
Both can be useful.

14
00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:09.171
Primary sources reduce distance from the evidence, while secondary sources may provide context or explanation.

15
00:01:09.171 --> 00:01:16.738
Critical thinkers know which kind they are using and avoid treating interpretation as if it were the original record.

16
00:01:16.738 --> 00:01:18.190
Data quality matters.

17
00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:26.904
Ask how the data was collected, what the sample represents, what might be missing, and whether the measure actually supports the claim.

18
00:01:26.904 --> 00:01:34.394
At a high level, sample size affects reliability, but size alone does not fix biased collection or poor measurement.

19
00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:41.044
Cherry-picking occurs when only convenient evidence is shown while conflicting evidence is ignored.

20
00:01:41.044 --> 00:01:44.560
AI-generated material deserves the same discipline.

21
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:52.738
Fluent output can be useful for drafting, summarizing, brainstorming, or comparing possibilities, but fluency is not truth.

22
00:01:52.738 --> 00:01:56.789
AI output can be incomplete, unsupported, outdated, or wrong.

23
00:01:56.789 --> 00:01:59.999
Treat it as material to examine, not as authority.

24
00:01:59.999 --> 00:02:05.043
Verify important claims against appropriate sources before relying on them.
